                <pb facs="0578" n="480" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg480"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3537: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Crane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName> [<date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                            notAfter="1920-10-12T10:33:00-04:00"
                            notBefore="1920-10-10T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >undated</hi></date>].<lb/>[Received October 12, 1920—10:33
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>314. Following from Canton October 10, 4 p.m.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>Referring to my telegram of October 4, 5 p.m.<anchor n="47" xml:id="d475-f1"
                        /> Tang Ting Kwang was installed at Honam Friday by his partisans as
                        military governor. Mo remains master of the situation and refuses to resign
                        his position, grows stronger daily, probability of fighting less imminent.
                        British Legation requested to arrange peace between North and South. Kindly
                        inform the Department. Bergholz.</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Crane</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
